{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '10414,10471p'\nnl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '101,218p'\nnl -ba docs/prd/06-views.md | sed -n '72,145p'\"","aggregated_output":" 10414\t## D693 · 2026-11-21 · Under Alex's A (D691): the design for linking related questions reaches council consensus at $0; three choices go to Alex before any build or send; the self-made reference is traced\n 10415\t\n 10416\t- **Source of authority:** Alex's \"A\" (D691) and D692's registered next step (\"the dependency design review reopens at $0 ... then goes\n 10417\t  to Alex before any build or send\"); Alex, 2026-11-21: \"好继续\".\n 10418\t- **Council R** (`docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/RESULT.md`; Codex thread `01a1fcdb…`, rounds 1–3;\n 10419\t  round 2 DISAGREE with five changes, round 3 DISAGREE on one, all taken; consensus): for the product (poc-v118, no seed edges) a\n 10420\t  build-ready design is reachable after Alex's choices and a $0 proof. The matching step chooses links (D51, D368 item 2) through one\n 10421\t  three-way matcher question that also covers an existing question reading a newly opened one (D191 point 2 at opening; old-2 opens\n 10422\t  Data Center before total revenue); a link between two already open questions stays a separate slice. The matcher answers the\n 10423\t  relation's sign and code maps the label from typed rung bounds. The reader is shown the read judgement's comparison point and its\n 10424\t  forecast in full, labelled as the system's, beside a bounded reasons excerpt. A chosen link moves no statement (106 items linked to\n 10425\t  both questions in old-2 are exposed to literal D265). D542's five items are settled for the product as written in the record.\n 10426\t- **Withdrawn by Claude in round 1:** a link written by code from typed keys (a hand list over measure names, D167, AGENTS §2). Corrected\n 10427\t  by Codex in round 2: July already holds an outside total-revenue estimate (Bloomberg-compiled $393B, e195), so July alone is the first\n 10428\t  send. Corrected in round 3: both questions are judged fresh in every sequence.\n 10429\t- **The self-made reference** (`docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/self-reference/RESULT.md`, `reference_census.py`, $0): 44 of\n 10430\t  2,375 number judgements (1.9%) present the system's own earlier figure as the reference, 33 of them on the FY2027 Data Center question\n 10431\t  (old-1, old-2); mechanism traced on old-2 v9 07-06 to 09-20: the previous conclusion is shown as rung and reasons only, without the\n 10432\t  source of its figures, and no sentence of judging-v7 covers \"no market figure supplied\"; the Wall Street estimate sits under total\n 10433\t  revenue only. Its own fix (a sourced reference) returns to Alex separately, after the link is measured.\n 10434\t- **Decided (council consensus; Alex's choices pending):** nothing is built or sent. To Alex: (a) a chosen reading link leaves filing\n 10435\t  alone; (b) open new matching and judging versions for the shared mechanism, the first test accepting only the existing-reader /\n 10436\t  new-target case; (c) after the $0 proof passes the council, the July comparison (old-2, 07-10 to 07-12, three linked and three\n 10437\t  control sequences, both questions fresh, pass rule in `codex-r2.md` §7(b)), cap $0.15, estimate $0.06–0.07. Recommendation: all three.\n 10438\t\n 10439\t## D694 · 2026-11-21 · Alex chooses A: links leave filing alone; new matching and judging versions open for linking; the July test after the council passes the $0 proof\n 10440\t\n 10441\t- **Alex's words** (2026-11-21), replying to the three choices of D693: \"A\". The options were:\n 10442\t  - **A**, recommended: (a) a chosen reading link leaves evidence filing alone (D265 applies only to a stated hierarchy); (b) open new\n 10443\t    matching and judging versions for the shared mechanism (both opening orders; the first test accepts only the existing-reader /\n 10444\t    new-target case); (c) after the $0 proof has passed the council, the July comparison (old-2, 07-10 to 07-12, three linked and three\n 10445\t    control sequences, both questions fresh, pass rule `dependency-design/codex-r2.md` §7(b)), cap $0.15, estimate $0.06–0.07;\n 10446\t  - **B**, the code and the $0 proof only, asking again before the paid test;\n 10447\t  - **C**, not now.\n 10448\t- **Decided (Alex):** A. poc-v118 stays the product and the control; the linking version is a new configuration. The sourced-reference\n 10449\t  change is not opened (it returns after the July measurement, D693).\n 10450\t- **Next:** the $0 work of D693 in order (tables and request audit; the July control rebuilt by request identity and filing replayed;\n 10451\t  real checks and writer with fixed answers for both orders and the listed events; built requests inspected, rule frozen, cap set),\n 10452\t  then the council on the proof, then the send.\n   101\t2. **Value × reader.** Every value a change writes, against every reader of it (found by searching the field) and every input source the\n   102\t   value can come from. A rule over a kind of data lists every writer of that kind. A change to how often something fires is counted\n   103\t   against every reader of its output.\n   104\t3. **New path × old checks.** For a new input, derived value, mode or changed data shape: every check the old path passes, and whether\n   105\t   the new one meets it. A changed shape re-derives every check written for the old one.\n   106\t4. **Prior decisions.** The decisions searched for the mechanism by name; any decision that rejected or bounded it is quoted in the\n   107\t   design and in the question to Alex.\n   108\t\n   109\t- A contract over text or shapes states the closed set of what passes (a full match); anything else fails or is not judged. Enumerate\n   110\t  the accepted set against every context and reader it meets before the first edit.\n   111\t- **Code owns identity, never importance.** Before building a closed list, enum or gate, write which decision it takes from the model\n   112\t  and whether that decision is identity (\"are these the same thing?\") or judgement (\"does this matter?\"); build only the identity part.\n   113\t  A list of what matters is at most a hint shown to the model (Alex, 2026-10-25: \"你这个做法难道不是非常的限死吗？永远都是一堆财务指标，真正的观点呢？\").\n   114\t- **No case-fitted fix.** A hand list, a special path or a rule fitted to the case in view fixes that case and breaks the next. First ask\n   115\t  whether the judge had the material and the definition it needed, and whether a typed field is missing; run the proposal against the\n   116\t  existing tests and the gold for a counter-example before building (D167: \"我不要 hardcode 解法 it's premature optimization, not general root fix\").\n   117\t- A blind review measures the tables. A serious finding they do not hold shows a missing dimension: add it and walk every row again,\n   118\t  never answer the finding alone. Code starts when the review finds nothing serious. A design whose core contract is still open is a\n   119\t  question, not something to build.\n   120\t- A foundation's acceptance carries a size and speed budget from its first control, not only correctness.\n   121\t\n   122\t## 3. Writing code\n   123\t\n   124\t- **One owner.** A rule lives where the data is written, and refuses: a warning that lets the run continue is a silent failure. Fix the\n   125\t  writer and delete its duplicate implementations in the same change; when a fix lands in one consumer, search every consumer of the\n   126\t  same key or rule.\n   127\t- **Code over prose.** A contract that code or a schema can enforce (an enum over allowed keys, a shape) is enforced there, and stays out\n   128\t  of prompts and documents.\n   129\t- **One word per concept.** The glossary in `docs/prd/00-system.md` (section Glossary; D36, D163) names each concept once, and\n   130\t  `docs/check.py` fails on a word it rules out. A new concept gets its row and its ruled-out words in the same change; a new ruled-out\n   131\t  word must catch the sentence it was written against and pass the corrected one.\n   132\t- **Identity.** An identifier names one thing for the life of its scope; a changed thing gets a new id. Equal values alone never\n   133\t  establish identity (D248 boundary 7, the same statement carried by several outlets, is a chosen exception, switched on only after its\n   134\t  merge risk on the gold and the accuracy of the original speaker and day fields are measured).\n   135\t- **Explicit inputs.** Library code takes runtime paths and configuration as arguments. A branch a run takes comes from its\n   136\t  configuration, never from whether an environment variable happens to be set; a configured step whose credential is missing refuses\n   137\t  the run. A component that sends a model call uses the transport its caller passes.\n   138\t- **Never order by the wall clock.** It steps both ways; order comes from a sequence the program owns: dispatch's sequence number. The\n   139\t  stream never reads the calendar; only pull mode acts on time, with its \"now\" passed in (D12, D13, D31).\n   140\t- **Strictness is code too.** A check added to prevent defects can itself fail; accept it on real data, not samples.\n   141\t- **Style.** Descriptive names. Docstrings and comments state behaviour, not history. The standard library unless a measured need\n   142\t  justifies a dependency.\n   143\t- **Frontend presentation.** Follow the sentence-case and metadata-separator rules in `docs/poc/thesis-ui.md#presentation-rules`.\n   144\t\n   145\t## 4. Tests\n   146\t\n   147\t- Every test names the requirement or table row it proves; a test that traces to none is deleted, and a retired behaviour's tests leave\n   148\t  with it. The suite stays lean.\n   149\t- Before writing a test, read every function its fixture drives: return shapes, failure classification, the data and attributes it\n   150\t  reads. Check that a name exists before calling it.\n   151\t- Real objects first (real storage on a temporary path, real records). Fake only external services (a model client, a data source, an\n   152\t  HTTP server). Fixture values are realistic (real ISO timestamps, not placeholders).\n   153\t- Tests never reach a paid service or load a credential: `tests/conftest.py` replaces the model and Jev key readers with a refusal, so a\n   154\t  call that escapes its fake transport fails the test.\n   155\t- Write the one decisive assertion that fails without the code under test, and predict the run before running it. A first failure for\n   156\t  any other reason means the test was written blind: stop and read again. A test is never mended to fit the code; it changes only when\n   157\t  the spec changes, and the spec change is recorded first.\n   158\t- Mutate on a scratch copy: the test must fail on the mutant while the unmutated control passes. An error is not a catch until its\n   159\t  traceback is read. A negative assertion (\"never called\") needs a mutant that makes the call on the tested path.\n   160\t- A check must judge something: it reports how many cases it judged, carries a positive control that fails for the breach it holds, and\n   161\t  fails when its own preconditions fail (exit status, a missing or unidentifiable input). A check over an empty population is\n   162\t  unexercised, not passed.\n   163\t- Tests never depend on the real date or the working directory: pin every date written and read, build paths from the test file's\n   164\t  location, write only inside temporary directories.\n   165\t- Test each entry point as its user calls it (with the defaults the user leaves unset, under the configuration the user runs), not only\n   166\t  the inner function with every argument set.\n   167\t\n   168\t## 5. Verification\n   169\t\n   170\tVerify at the point the change touches, at the sharpest level that shows it works or fails, and stop there:\n   171\t1. Stored data: reproduce the failing case, show the fix flips it, show a mutant of the fix fails the check ($0).\n   172\t2. Only when what the model is asked changed: re-send just the affected calls.\n   173\t3. The touched suites, plus any standing structural checks the change can affect; the full suite once.\n   174\t4. The gate once, at merge (README, \"Build and check\"). The acceptance once, on the final configuration, scored on the frozen ruler\n   175\t   (§7.1). Never a full rerun per fix.\n   176\t\n   177\t- A further check the change does not need is offered in one line, not run. After the checks that prove a change pass, report and close.\n   178\t- To accept a code change, replay stored decisions through the code deterministically ($0); a sampler run proves nothing about code,\n   179\t  and a cached replay proves nothing about the model.\n   180\t- A re-sent request is the bytes production sends, not the archive's canonical JSON: `model.request_identity` sorts keys for the\n   181\t  archive, `model.call` sends the dict as built. Check a harness by comparing an answer's key order with a production answer's.\n   182\t- Before any paid run, `python3 scripts/preflight.py` replays the stored model answers and matching records of the archives and ledgers\n   183\t  it is given through the current code checks and prompts ($0; it skips rows an earlier run refused, so read what it says it skipped)\n   184\t  and prints, per check, the cases judged and the refusals with reasons (it does not yet replay the coverage-start reading's check:\n   185\t  `CURRENT_STATE.md`, open defects). A changed share or a refusal on a case a person would accept is\n   186\t  diagnosed before the run; a paid run is never the way a check or a prompt is first tried.\n   187\t- An acceptance round is a measurement of the final configuration, not a verification step. A new finding in it is recorded with its\n   188\t  census, not answered with another round.\n   189\t- The product runs end to end before anything else is built, and every merge is accepted on the product's own path. A gate passes only\n   190\t  what it runs: name the check that runs a new path, or say that none does.\n   191\t- Measure before claiming. Label estimates as estimates, report every sample, and treat a number in a design document as a measurement\n   192\t  with a script behind it.\n   193\t\n   194\t## 6. Writing prompts\n   195\t\n   196\t- **The prompt carries the standard, not new rules.** Every sentence about what to keep, reject, weigh or open traces to a line of the\n   197\t  step's standard (§7.1) or a decision; a sentence with no such line is a proposal for Alex, not an edit. A reject sentence must not\n   198\t  remove what the standard keeps: a missing amount, date or scale is never on its own a reason to drop a statement an analyst would note;\n   199\t  its weight is judged apart and may be lower (D240, Alex: \"我不是和你说过不要这么死板吗？这个证据显然是有意义的，不一定什么条件数据时间都要满足，你应该把它记录下来，只是可能档位低一点\").\n   200\t- **The prompt audit runs after every measurement and before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`). The whole\n   201\t  request (system, every category block, the fixed user sentences, every schema description and example) is read against the standard\n   202\t  clause by clause, in both directions, with a blind read-only second reader: each clause is consistent, contradicts, keeps wider, keeps\n   203\t  narrower or has no source. The procedure is audited too: a rule that decides by another test than the standard's, or an exception\n   204\t  written wider than the standard scopes it, contradicts the standard even when every sentence quotes it. Each failure class of the last\n   205\t  measurement gets one row (the request sentence, the standard's line, a verdict: contradiction, missing, abstract, misread, not the\n   206\t  prompt) in the arm's design document before a fix is written; a fix that answers a bench miss rather than a sentence is not written.\n   207\t  Alex, 2026-10-30: \"你需要同时仔细检查prompt和判法准则矛盾的问题，出现很多次了，你应该要做很细的检查\"; 2026-11-02: \"这个就是prompt的问题，你有没有深度的看promt 然后映射问题找矛盾\n   208\t  … 你应该看着prompt，然后对比失败的trace，然后仔细的根据尺子做对比，并且我们prompt的principle 也要带入，是尝试教他general hedge fund\n   209\t  analyst skill, 不是要hardcode或是benchmark farming；你把这个写成一个skill 以后固定跑，不然你每次都没有检讨到\".\n   210\t- **A test the model must apply to each statement is a check at the very end of the same prompt.** In one pass the model keeps what\n   211\t  looks like evidence and cites a rule afterwards; asked afterwards it judges correctly. So a closing check reads the list back and\n   212\t  returns only what passes, in the same call: the standard's own test, then its not-kept rows, each with its own boundary and the\n   213\t  standard's own examples, audited like any prompt text; a lost entry is judged by a blind reader. Alex, 2026-10-30: \"这个完全就是再prompt 的最后面要求做完一遍基于'你刚才说的复核条件'再跑一次；写在同一个提示词里面\".\n   214\t- **A prompt serves every company, never the bench.** Every analyst prompt is a general hedge-fund brain: each rule and example reads the\n   215\t  same for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip maker. Examples teach from varied domains; none names the covered company, its\n   216\t  products or its numbers, and none is taken from a test article. A fix is written from the standard and from the request read as the\n   217\t  model reads it, not from the bench's misses, and a prompt is scrubbed for one-company or one-industry wording before any measurement.\n   218\t  Alex, 2026-10-29 (D237): \"It needs to be general purpose as a hedge fund brain. I keep on telling you this, and you keep on cherry\n    72\t    ├─ Rung 4 → Licences required and issued; all unconditional\n    73\t    └─ Rung 5 → Licences no longer required\n    74\t```\n    75\t\n    76\t1. A ladder has five rungs. Asset theses run from strongly bullish to strongly bearish, describing business impact; theme theses name five ordered outcomes without good/bad labels. Use two rungs for yes/no rather than forcing five: bullish/bearish for assets; named outcomes for themes, such as signed by the deadline/not signed. Decided for the next extraction version (D186, D368): asset rungs state the business outcome against guidance (above, near, below) and each thesis names its favourable end.\n    77\t2. Each rung is verifiable: a numeric interval or event stage. Rungs must be exhaustive and non-overlapping, so exactly one applies at expiry. For example: up at least 50%; up 10% to under 50%; change under 10% either way; down 10% to under 80%; down at least 80%. Every boundary belongs to one rung. “Up a lot/a little” cannot be checked. Avoid vague partial outcomes; specify the partial state, such as conditional licences, as a distinct rung. State the comparison underlying neutral.\n    78\t3. One ladder concerns one thing. Separate numeric and event questions and connect them with dependencies. Likewise, separate components that can move in opposite directions, such as H20 and Blackwell chip classes.\n    79\t4. The extraction model writes the candidate's ladder. A new thesis adopts it unchanged until expiry. A merged candidate uses the existing thesis's ladder and due date.\n    80\t5. Numeric theses name a year. Non-company quantities use calendar years: the publication year is this year and the following year is next year, each ending December 31. Company quantities use the fiscal year containing publication day and its successor. Never use the computer clock. For a given quantity, open at most one thesis for each of this year and next year; do not open later years. The due date is the quantity's publication date.\n    81\t6. Set the due date when the debate can be resolved, usually 6–12 months away. Intermediate quarterly reports are evidence, not separate theses.\n    82\t<!-- /generated: ladder -->\n    83\t\n    84\t## State\n    85\t\n    86\t<!-- generated: positions -->\n    87\t|  | Meaning | Decision maker | Example |\n    88\t|---|---|---|---|\n    89\t| State | Our judgement of the thesis's current rung; evidence pushes toward either end | The model judges the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, against a reference (below). It reads the evidence in view, the arrivals since its fifth most recent judgement and the basis its last ten judgements cited, the request naming how many more pieces stay in force (D198); a revision is shown beside the piece it revises and a repeat beside the piece the matcher read it as, both in force (D202, D204); plus current dependency states and reasons and, for an asset thesis, the theme block. Do not increment or decrement the previous score. Cite evidence and compare with the conclusion before this round to state whether it changed. Do not rejudge if only source references were added and dependency conclusions did not change. | After the 8-K: strongly bearish |\n    90\t\n    91\t- Every judgement states its basis in a fixed response shape: evidence IDs such as e12 and e15, plus dependency thesis IDs such as #1 when their changes triggered rejudgement.\n    92\t- Code checks cited IDs against the request that was sent: evidence must be among the pieces shown to this thesis (D198); cited theses must be its dependencies. Accept only if all checks pass. Otherwise record the reason and ask for one corrected judgement. If that also fails, use normal failure handling: retry from this step, then human review after repeated failure. This checks references, not whether the judgement is correct, like verifying a cited page exists.\n    93\t- Each judgement also declares whether the conclusion changed and explains why, comparing with the state and reasons before this round, including during retries. That earlier conclusion is for comparison, not incremental scoring. A rung change or a material change in the supporting facts/numbers counts: AMZN capex guidance moving from 12% to 25% growth changes the conclusion even within one rung. Repetition does not. A changed conclusion triggers dependent theses in the same round. Code requires changed=true for a first judgement or rung change; otherwise refuse under the same correction rules.\n    94\t- For historical judgement, read the records saved on that date. Do not ask the model again; a new sample can differ.\n    95\t- The rung is a forecast against a reference (D260): the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, judged against what the market expects (consensus, guidance, the stated plan or deadline). The judgement names the reference and its rung and the outcome the evidence implies (a figure or date derived from reported figures, guidance and quantified revisions), and moves when the implied outcome crosses a rung boundary. A consensus figure alone keeps the judgement at the reference's rung and is not the reason to stay once other evidence has arrived; \"not established\" is confidence, not a reason to stay. From judging-v6 the reference and the implied outcome are required fields of the answer, stored beside the rung; an empty one is refused.\n    96\t<!-- /generated: positions -->\n    97\t\n    98\t## Four evidence tiers\n    99\t\n   100\t<!-- generated: tiers -->\n   101\t| Tier | Example | Role |\n   102\t|---|---|---|\n   103\t| Fact | Official actions, published figures and completed transactions | Fixes what is known; the forecast starts from it |\n   104\t| Party statement | Company guidance, management statements and official speeches | The strongest input to the forecast |\n   105\t| Third-party view | Sell-side analysts, media analysis and experts | Moves the forecast in proportion to its specificity |\n   106\t| Rumour | Unnamed-source claims and unattributed statements | Moves the forecast in proportion to its specificity |\n   107\t\n   108\t- The model determines the size of the push and gives reasons, by tier and novelty: a repeat, another outlet or an opinion with no new fact or calculation moves nothing (D260). No tier has a fixed score.\n   109\t- Count independent original sources. Twenty outlets retelling one exclusive are twenty pieces, each related to the one it repeats (D204), and one source, not twenty independent confirmations (D30).\n   110\t- Tier follows who confirmed it. An unnamed-source report that Commerce is issuing licences is a rumour. An official or company confirmation is factual evidence recorded as a revision of it, both in force (D202). The rumour stays a rumour.\n   111\t- Store evidence once without an intrinsic direction. Each rejudgement writes its push on the evidence–thesis link. One statement can affect multiple theses differently.\n   112\t<!-- /generated: tiers -->\n   113\t\n   114\t## Dependencies: reading the arrows\n   115\t\n   116\t<!-- generated: dependencies -->\n   117\t```\n   118\tB depends on A | What kind of thesis is A? (For an asset thesis B, state which end of A is bullish for B. For a theme thesis B, state whether their ladders have the same or opposite orientation.)\n   119\t├─ Numeric thesis → Read the specified year's thesis (Example: JPM 2027 net interest income → Fed year-end 2027 rate)\n   120\t│   ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons\n   121\t│   ├─ A is closed → Read A's closing state and reasons (The frozen outcome is what B needs; keep reading it)\n   122\t│   └─ A does not exist yet → Wait for it (Record the awaited year on the chain containing the shown thesis. When that year's successor is created, bind the dependency and rejudge B once.)\n   123\t└─ Event thesis → Follow succession (Example: NVDA FY2027 China revenue → chip export licensing to China)\n   124\t    ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons\n   125\t    ├─ A is closed with a successor → Read the successor (Record the change. The successor changes B's input, so rejudge B once.)\n   126\t    └─ A is closed without a successor → Read A's closing state and reasons, marked “historical only”\n   127\t```\n   128\t\n   129\t- Theme theses do not judge outcomes as good or bad. Each dependent asset thesis records its direction: rate cuts are bullish for Ethereum and bearish for JPM.\n   130\t- A dependency has one direction. An apparent “good first, bad later” relationship needs an intermediate thesis: steel tariffs raising domestic steel prices and weakening downstream steel demand are separate paths.\n   131\t- Dependencies and the theme block are the two ways theses influence each other; succession only orders periods. B depending on A means B reads A's conclusion. The theme block (D191, D195) shows every open, judged theme thesis to every asset thesis's judgement, one to all, and the judgement states which way each theme it weighed pushes it; no edge is written for it. Keep B's direct evidence separate from dependency states and reasons. A's conclusion can affect B like evidence but is not stored as evidence: external events and statements are evidence, whereas A is our own judgement. Treating it as evidence would let the system convince itself.\n   132\t- Count a statement once. A statement about A is A's evidence, including when two candidates arise from one item. B learns it through A's conclusion. Daily filing gives a statement one home: the narrowest open thesis it bears on, as that thesis's own figure, step or estimate or through the demand, supply, competition or pricing behind its answer, plus the same answer's candidates (D207, D259, D264, D265); the separate coverage-start reading was retired (D370). Dependencies are chosen only when B is created; a later item adding a dependency to an existing thesis is decided (D191 point 2) and to be built (D368).\n   133\t- Rejudge an open thesis when its inputs change: new evidence, replacement, a new link to existing evidence, a dependency declaring a changed conclusion, or (for an asset thesis) a theme thesis declaring one. Additional source references alone do not count. Binding an awaited A or moving to its successor also changes B's inputs. Closed theses do not update. A memo-derived thesis is first judged when evidence first links to it; before that, it and its dependants see “not yet judged”.\n   134\t- The matching call chooses a new thesis's dependencies, stating which conclusions it relies on and each direction (D51). Choose only among searched theses, extraction block-5 theses and candidates opened from the same item; no extra search. Jev, the default matcher, is not asked for them, so new theses do not receive dependencies through the default matcher; building the choice on the Jev path is decided (D368). The historical seed questions are retired (D370). A historical seed's dependency arrow means \"supports\" (D368, by D208): for filing, the supporting (driver) thesis is the narrower one; for judging, the supported (root) thesis reads the supporting theses' conclusions, so drivers and themes are judged before their roots. The judging code reads the stored edge the other way today; the fix is to be built and measured (D368). Completed events are evidence, not dependencies: today's oil price influencing OPEC output is evidence for the OPEC-output thesis.\n   135\t- Before adding an edge or following it to a successor, code checks for cycles. Refuse a cycle and ask the model to correct it; persistent cycles follow normal failure handling. Apparently mutually dependent questions indicate poorly defined theses.\n   136\t<!-- /generated: dependencies -->\n   137\t\n   138\t## Succession: the same focus over successive periods\n   139\t\n   140\t<!-- generated: chain -->\n   141\t- Successive periods of the same focus form a chain, such as Fed year-end 2026 rate → 2027 rate → 2028 rate. The chain records period order. Dependency traversal follows the rules above.\n   142\t- One thesis has at most one successor: FY2026 cannot produce two FY2027 successors.\n   143\t- Keep ladder orientation across succession: a strongly bullish first rung remains strongly bullish, although thresholds can change. Dependencies retain their direction; reversing the ends would break it.\n   144\t<!-- /generated: chain -->\n   145\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'p=docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design\nnl -ba \"$p/RESULT.md\"\nnl -ba \"$p/rebuild_control.py\"\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '\"'10432,10468p'\nnl -ba .claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'p=docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design\nnl -ba \"$p/RESULT.md\"\nnl -ba \"$p/rebuild_control.py\"\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '\"'10432,10468p'\nnl -ba .claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Council R: linking related questions in the product, the design and what goes to Alex (D692; 2026-11-21; $0)\n     2\t\n     3\tConsensus in round 3 (Codex thread `01a1fcdb-2f9a-7163-b7ac-e325b9b6cbe9`, read-only, memories off). Record: `brief.md`,\n     4\t`selection_census.py` → `selection-census.md`, `codex-r1.md`, `claude-r1.md` (written before reading Codex's), `round2-brief.md`,\n     5\t`codex-r2.md` (DISAGREE with five changes, all taken), `round3-brief.md`, `codex-r3.md` (DISAGREE on item 7 only: both questions fresh\n     6\tin every sequence; taken). With it: `../self-reference/RESULT.md` (the self-made market reference, traced). Nothing was built or sent.\n     7\t\n     8\t## Answer\n     9\tA build-ready design is reachable for the product (poc-v118, no seeds) after Alex's choices below and a $0 proof the council reviews;\n    10\tit is not build-ready yet. D542's \"not sound for build\" concerned the seeded reading; the product holds no seed edge.\n    11\t\n    12\t## What the council agreed\n    13\t1. **Who chooses a link.** The matching step, as D51 and D368 item 2 decide; the Jev matcher returns none today. One three-way matcher\n    14\t   question per new candidate and shown question, on final identities: the candidate reads the shown question; the shown question\n    15\t   reads the candidate; neither. The second outcome is D191 point 2's later addition at the moment the read question opens (old-2\n    16\t   opens Data Center at seq 62 and total revenue at seq 148). Code checks endpoints, identities, circles and conflicts. Claude's\n    17\t   round-1 proposal (code writes a link from typed keys) was withdrawn: a rule over measure names is a hand list (D167; AGENTS §2), and\n    18\t   the same relation holds for aggregates against single deals and capex totals. The matcher's existing \"part_of\" answer is\n    19\t   undirected and noisy (`selection-census.md`) and cannot choose. A link between two questions that both already exist is a separate\n    20\t   slice.\n    21\t2. **Direction and label.** The reason for the link decides who reads whom; for a segment and its company total, the segment reads\n    22\t   the total (PRD 06, D537, the probe). D368 item 4 concerns seed driver edges. The matcher answers the sign of the relation; code maps\n    23\t   it to `first_is_bullish` / `last_is_bullish` (or `same` / `opposite` for a theme reader) from typed rung bounds and the reader's\n    24\t   favourable end, after any successor reversal, and refuses an undecidable mapping.\n    25\t3. **What the reader is shown.** The read judgement's `reference` as \"the comparison point stated by the judgement for [question]\"\n    26\t   and its `implied_outcome` as \"the system's forecast for [question]\", in full, beside a bounded reasons excerpt; no \"market\" label\n    27\t   (old-1 v1 carried its own base as a comparison point). Today's line prints reasons cut at 600 characters, which loses the decisive\n    28\t   figure in old-1 and old-3; `store.current_state` must return both fields.\n    29\t4. **Filing.** A chosen reading link moves no statement. Literal D265 would act on the **106** items linked to both Data Center and\n    30\t   total revenue in old-2 (exposure; the loss is measured by replaying filing); the Wall Street estimate items v14 cites are on v14\n    31\t   only. This reading of D265 is Alex's choice.\n    32\t5. **D542's five items, for the product:** the circle through a waiting link is refused and corrected per PRD 06 (the check runs on\n    33\t   the whole proposed set); closing keeps a closed number question's frozen answer, an event reads \"historical only\" until a\n    34\t   successor, recorded with the item; a theme linked by an asset question is shown once, in the theme block; historical readers pick\n    35\t   the latest visible target at their boundary (the service's and the bundle's reverse lookups need fixing); the seed label\n    36\t   `last_end` has no role.\n    37\t6. **Order and cost:** the read question is judged first; a changed conclusion queues its reader once that day; zero or one extra judge\n    38\t   call a day per pair, about $0.0056 each (estimate).\n    39\t7. **The two defects meet at a missing input and then diverge.** Linking delivers the whole's figures; the self-made reference also\n    40\t   comes from the previous conclusion's unmarked figures (two of three linked probe answers kept the carried $414B). One judging\n    41\t   version is opened for the link; the sourced-reference change returns to Alex separately, after measuring what the link leaves.\n    42\t\n    43\t## Alex's choices (recommendation: all three)\n    44\t- **(a)** A chosen reading link leaves evidence filing alone (D265 read as applying only to a stated hierarchy).\n    45\t- **(b)** Open new matching and judging versions for the shared mechanism (both opening orders); the first test accepts only the\n    46\t  existing-reader / new-target case.\n    47\t- **(c)** After the $0 proof has passed the council, the July comparison below, cap $0.15 (estimate $0.06–0.07).\n    48\t\n    49\t## The test (registered)\n    50\told-2, from just before the item at seq 148 (07-10) through 07-12. Real matching and writing must select and write \"Data Center reads\n    51\tthe newly opened total revenue\". Three linked and three control sequences from the same starting state; **every sequence has its own\n    52\tarchive for both questions**, so both are judged fresh and each day reads its own previous answers; unrelated calls replay only on an\n    53\texact request match. On 07-10..07-12 total revenue cites the Bloomberg-compiled $393B (e195); Data Center's requests hold neither it\n    54\tnor total revenue's forecast (checked). Pass rule: `codex-r2.md` section 7(b), marks A–D (comparable answers; arithmetic and sources;\n    55\troom for the rest of NVIDIA, at least $6B, the Q1 floor; the link did the work), pass / inconclusive / fail, with the harm check on\n    56\tboth questions. A cap stop before six sequences finish is inconclusive.\n    57\t\n    58\t## $0 work before the send, in order (after (a) and (b))\n    59\t1. The four AGENTS §2 tables completed; the exact matching and judging requests audited; endpoints, signs, labels and correction\n    60\t   outcomes written.\n    61\t2. The July control rebuilt by exact request identity; filing replayed over the 106-item overlap, with D538's seven-link case and an\n    62\t   unrelated pair as controls.\n    63\t3. Real matching checks and writing with fixed answers for both opening orders: a circle through a waiting link, a conflicting\n    64\t   label, close and successor, a theme shown once, historical reads at the transition, resume after a partial round.\n    65\t4. The built July requests inspected (the $393B outside estimate kept apart from the forecast; old-1's credit-support case; a change of\n    66\t   comparison point with the forecast and rung fixed); the reading rule frozen; the cap set from the rebuilt requests.\n     1\t\"\"\"$0 step 2 of D694: rebuild old-2's ledger as it stood at the end of 2026-07-09 (the July test's starting state) by replaying the\n     2\tproduct (poc-v118) from 2026-07-01 over old-2's own stored answers. Every request must be answered from the archive: the transport\n     3\trefuses any send, so a request that differs from the stored one by a byte stops the rebuild. The archive is a scratch copy (a\n     4\trefused judge answer is retired in the copy only).\n     5\t\n     6\t  python3 rebuild_control.py <scratch folder>\n     7\t\n     8\tThen it compares the rebuilt ledger with old-2's finished store up to the end of 07-09: views, keys, rungs, evidence, links and\n     9\tjudgements (rung, reasons, request sha) must be equal.\n    10\t\"\"\"\n    11\timport shutil\n    12\timport sqlite3\n    13\timport sys\n    14\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    15\t\n    16\tREPO = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4]\n    17\tsys.path.insert(0, str(REPO / 'src'))\n    18\tfrom hivemind import config, model, replay, universe        # noqa: E402\n    19\t\n    20\tOLD2 = REPO / 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-2/prod'\n    21\tITEMS = REPO / 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/items-comparison.sqlite'\n    22\tUNTIL = '2026-07-09'\n    23\t\n    24\t\n    25\tclass SendRefused(RuntimeError):\n    26\t    pass\n    27\t\n    28\t\n    29\tdef refuse(*args, **kwargs):\n    30\t    raise SendRefused('a request was not in the archive: the rebuilt request differs from the stored one')\n    31\t\n    32\t\n    33\tdef rows(conn, sql, args=()):\n    34\t    return [tuple(r) for r in conn.execute(sql, args)]\n    35\t\n    36\t\n    37\tdef compare(rebuilt_path, last_seq):\n    38\t    rebuilt = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{rebuilt_path}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    39\t    stored = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{OLD2}/views.sqlite?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    40\t    checks = {\n    41\t        'views': ('select view_id, question, created_seq from views where created_seq <= ? order by view_id', (last_seq,)),\n    42\t        'view_keys': ('select view_id, measure, scope, period_start, period_end, unit from view_keys where seq <= ? order by view_id', (last_seq,)),\n    43\t        'evidence': ('select evidence_id, quote, created_seq from evidence where created_seq <= ? order by evidence_id', (last_seq,)),\n    44\t        'evidence_links': ('select evidence_id, view_id, seq from evidence_links where seq <= ? order by evidence_id, view_id', (last_seq,)),\n    45\t        'judgements': ('select view_id, seq, rung, reasons, request_sha256 from judgements where seq <= ? order by judgement_id', (last_seq,)),\n    46\t        'dependencies': ('select view_id, depends_on, seq from dependencies where seq <= ? order by dependency_id', (last_seq,)),\n    47\t    }\n    48\t    ok = True\n    49\t    for name, (sql, args) in checks.items():\n    50\t        a, b = rows(rebuilt, sql, args), rows(stored, sql, args)\n    51\t        same = a == b\n    52\t        ok = ok and same\n    53\t        print(f'{name}: rebuilt {len(a)} stored {len(b)} {\"equal\" if same else \"DIFFER\"}')\n    54\t        if not same:\n    55\t            for x, y in zip(a, b):\n    56\t                if x != y:\n    57\t                    print('  first difference:', str(x)[:200], '|', str(y)[:200])\n    58\t                    break\n    59\t    return ok\n    60\t\n    61\t\n    62\tdef main():\n    63\t    scratch = Path(sys.argv[1])\n    64\t    scratch.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    65\t    views_path, archive_path = scratch / 'views.sqlite', scratch / 'archive.sqlite'\n    66\t    for path in (views_path, archive_path):\n    67\t        if path.exists():\n    68\t            raise SystemExit(f'{path} exists: a rebuild starts from an empty folder')\n    69\t    shutil.copyfile(OLD2 / 'archive.sqlite', archive_path)\n    70\t    cfg = config.load(str(REPO / 'poc/config/poc-v118.json'))\n    71\t    uni = universe.load(str(REPO / 'poc/universe.json'))\n    72\t    totals = replay.run(testdb_path=str(ITEMS), views_path=str(views_path), archive=model.Archive(str(archive_path)), cfg=cfg,\n    73\t                        universe=uni, from_day='2026-07-01', until_day=UNTIL, transport=refuse, log=print,\n    74\t                        only_objects={'NVDA'})          # the comparison launcher's default (`budget_replay.py --only NVDA`)\n    75\t    print('totals:', totals)\n    76\t    conn = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{views_path}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    77\t    last_seq = conn.execute('select max(seq) from days_completed where day=?', (UNTIL,)).fetchone()[0]\n    78\t    print('last completed seq at', UNTIL, last_seq)\n    79\t    sys.exit(0 if compare(views_path, last_seq) else 1)\n    80\t\n    81\t\n    82\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n    83\t    main()\n 10432\t  source of its figures, and no sentence of judging-v7 covers \"no market figure supplied\"; the Wall Street estimate sits under total\n 10433\t  revenue only. Its own fix (a sourced reference) returns to Alex separately, after the link is measured.\n 10434\t- **Decided (council consensus; Alex's choices pending):** nothing is built or sent. To Alex: (a) a chosen reading link leaves filing\n 10435\t  alone; (b) open new matching and judging versions for the shared mechanism, the first test accepting only the existing-reader /\n 10436\t  new-target case; (c) after the $0 proof passes the council, the July comparison (old-2, 07-10 to 07-12, three linked and three\n 10437\t  control sequences, both questions fresh, pass rule in `codex-r2.md` §7(b)), cap $0.15, estimate $0.06–0.07. Recommendation: all three.\n 10438\t\n 10439\t## D694 · 2026-11-21 · Alex chooses A: links leave filing alone; new matching and judging versions open for linking; the July test after the council passes the $0 proof\n 10440\t\n 10441\t- **Alex's words** (2026-11-21), replying to the three choices of D693: \"A\". The options were:\n 10442\t  - **A**, recommended: (a) a chosen reading link leaves evidence filing alone (D265 applies only to a stated hierarchy); (b) open new\n 10443\t    matching and judging versions for the shared mechanism (both opening orders; the first test accepts only the existing-reader /\n 10444\t    new-target case); (c) after the $0 proof has passed the council, the July comparison (old-2, 07-10 to 07-12, three linked and three\n 10445\t    control sequences, both questions fresh, pass rule `dependency-design/codex-r2.md` §7(b)), cap $0.15, estimate $0.06–0.07;\n 10446\t  - **B**, the code and the $0 proof only, asking again before the paid test;\n 10447\t  - **C**, not now.\n 10448\t- **Decided (Alex):** A. poc-v118 stays the product and the control; the linking version is a new configuration. The sourced-reference\n 10449\t  change is not opened (it returns after the July measurement, D693).\n 10450\t- **Next:** the $0 work of D693 in order (tables and request audit; the July control rebuilt by request identity and filing replayed;\n 10451\t  real checks and writer with fixed answers for both orders and the listed events; built requests inspected, rule frozen, cap set),\n 10452\t  then the council on the proof, then the send.\n     1\t---\n     2\tname: prompt-audit\n     3\tdescription: After every measurement of an extraction configuration, and before any prompt change, map each failure class in the stored draws to the sentence of the request that produced it, check that sentence against the frozen standard and the prompt principles, and write the audit table. Alex, 2026-11-02 - \"你应该看着prompt，然后对比失败的trace，然后仔细的根据尺子做对比，并且我们prompt的principle也要带入，是尝试教他general hedge fund analyst skill，不是要hardcode或是benchmark farming；你把这个写成一个skill以后固定跑\".\n     4\t---\n     5\t\n     6\t# Prompt audit: failures → the request's sentences → the standard → the principle\n     7\t\n     8\tRun this after every measured configuration (three draws scored on the frozen ruler) and before any edit to a prompt file, a schema\n     9\tdescription or a takes line. A measurement without this audit is a score without a diagnosis (AGENTS §7.2, §6). The audit is read,\n    10\tnot scripted: the scripts below only produce the lists to read.\n    11\t\n    12\t## 0. Inputs\n    13\t\n    14\t- The arm's run folders (`docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/arms/<arm>/d1..3`) and the baseline's.\n    15\t- The frozen ruler (AGENTS §7.1): the extraction standard `docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html` (version 3, D243, with D250's\n    16\t  boundaries), golden-v5, the questions standard, the judgement gold. Never edited; every disagreement is a gap in the system.\n    17\t- The prompt principles (AGENTS §6 and §8): the prompt carries the standard and no new rule; it teaches a general hedge-fund\n    18\t  analyst, so every sentence and example reads the same for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip maker, names no covered\n    19\t  company, product or number, and takes no example from a test article; a classification is a closed set with examples; never grow a\n    20\t  prompt to fix quality without cutting as much elsewhere; a field's definition sits in the schema beside the field; code owns\n    21\t  identity, never importance.\n    22\t\n    23\t## 1. Dump the request as the model reads it\n    24\t\n    25\tOne stored request of the configuration (any article), printed whole: the system text (the role), every user block before the item\n    26\t(category block, subjects, research reference, open questions with their takes lines, research groups), the closing sentence, the\n    27\tend-of-request check, and every schema field description (evidence and candidates). Read it top to bottom once. List every\n    28\tinstruction that decides keep, reject, link, open, or a field value, with its location (role paragraph, check row, schema field,\n    29\ttakes line, category block). This list is the thing the failures are mapped onto.\n    30\t\n    31\t```sh\n    32\tpython3 - <<'PY'\n    33\timport sqlite3, json\n    34\ta = sqlite3.connect('file:<run folder>/archive.sqlite?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    35\treq = json.loads(a.execute(\"select request_json from model_calls where purpose='extract' and request_json like '%<title fragment>%' order by call_id desc limit 1\").fetchone()[0])\n    36\tprint(req['messages'][0]['content']); print(req['messages'][1]['content'].split('[Item]')[0]); print(req['messages'][1]['content'].rsplit('\\n\\n', 2)[-2:])\n    37\tprint(json.dumps({k: v.get('description') for k, v in req['response_format']['json_schema']['schema']['properties']['evidence']['items']['properties'].items() if v.get('description')}, ensure_ascii=False, indent=1))\n    38\tPY\n    39\t```\n    40\t\n    41\t## 2. Produce the failure lists (all at no model cost, on the stored draws)\n    42\t\n    43\t| List | Script | What it gives |\n    44\t|---|---|---|\n    45\t| Matter states across draws | `harness/matter_states.py arm=d1,d2,d3 base=d1,d2,d3` | every gold core/supporting matter: extracted or reaching in 3/2/1/0 draws; the systematic misses (0 of 3) and the coin flips (1 of 3) |\n    46\t| Written noise by the standard's row | the `rows_all` count in `design-v72.md` §10 (the gold's `not_kept[].row`) | which row of the standard the written noise falls under, with examples |\n    47\t| No-home drops | `harness/dropped_census.py d1 d2 d3` | gold matters written but dropped, with the questions open at the time |\n    48\t| Where links go | `harness/links_by_question.py d1 d2 d3 --sample 20` | the pile per question, the revenue-sink share, statements under several questions, noise under revenue |\n    49\t| Quote refusals | the `dropped` reasons of the handovers (`quote not in item`), each diffed against its closest source window | paraphrase against a word or two off |\n    50\t| Question recall | `harness/question_recall.py d1 d2 d3` | must_open / may_open covered by seeds and by article-opened questions |\n    51\t| Fields | 15 random stored statements with speaker, original_who, original_day, tier | read against the quote and the item's day and publisher |\n    52\t\n    53\tFor every list, read the items, not the counts: the raw answer beside the article for every systematic miss (a miss the scorer reports\n    54\tmay be the matter written in other words: check before counting it), a sample of twenty for every pile.\n    55\t\n    56\t## 3. Map each failure class to the request\n    57\t\n    58\tFor each class with more than a handful of cases, fill one row:\n    59\t\n    60\t| Failure class (count, share) | The sentence(s) of the request that produced it, quoted | The standard's line on the same case (section, decision) | Verdict | The model's own account (when the sentence is not obvious) |\n    61\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n    62\t\n    63\tVerdicts, one of: **contradiction** (two sentences of the request pull opposite ways, or a request sentence removes what the standard\n    64\tkeeps, D240), **missing** (no sentence decides the case; the model guesses, a coin flip per send), **abstract** (a clause without an\n    65\texample, read differently each send), **misread** (the sentence is right, the model does not apply it; needs the model's own account\n    66\tbefore any change), **not the prompt** (code, scorer or sampler).\n    67\t\n    68\tThe model's own account: continue the stored conversation with one question about one case (`harness/probe_v72_link.py`,\n    69\t`probe_v72_missing.py`), a cent a case, for every \"misread\" and for any class whose sentence is not obvious from reading.\n    70\t\n    71\t## 4. Check every proposed fix against the principles before writing it\n    72\t\n    73\t- It is written from the standard's line, not from the failing article: the example comes from another domain, names no covered\n    74\t  company, product or number, and reads the same for every industry. A fix that only recovers the bench's misses is benchmark\n    75\t  farming and is not written.\n    76\t- It removes a contradiction, supplies a missing definition at its owner (schema field, takes line, check row, category block), or\n    77\t  replaces an abstract clause with a closed set and examples. It never adds a rule the standard does not hold.\n    78\t- It cuts at least as much as it adds, or the growth has a written reason; report characters before and after.\n    79\t- One class per change; the change is proved on the stored requests of that class, three sends as stored against three changed,\n    80\t  with the noise guards of the same articles; the whole bench accepts it (three draws) only when Alex asks for a run.\n    81\t\n    82\t## 5. Output\n    83\t\n    84\tWrite the table and the verdicts into the arm's design document (a numbered section), one row per class, in the order of the loss;\n    85\tthe report to Alex states each class in a reader's words (what the system does wrong, which sentence makes it do that, what the\n    86\tstandard says), never in the loop's own words (AGENTS, \"Authority and scope\": explain as to a sixteen-year-old).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
